    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "hey, i'm working on a loft addition for a client in oregon. we're using douglas fir for the main support. i need to figure out the span for a heavy beam to hold up the new floor, but i'm worried about deflection and if there are any specific load distribution rules i should be looking at for timber framing. what's the best way to calculate this to make sure the floor doesn't feel bouncy or sag over time?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "For the span calculation of a heavy timber beam in a lumber framing project, you'll need to consider the wood species (Douglas fir in your case), the grade and dimensions of the beam, and the load it needs to support, including the weight of the floor and any expected live loads. To minimize deflection and ensure structural integrity, consulting the American Wood Council (AWC) \"Engineered Wood Construction Design Specification\" will help you adhere to established building codes and engineering principles. You may also want to look into the use of floating or stiffened wood floor systems, as they are designed to handle heavy loads while minimizing deflection. Working with a structural engineer or a certified timber framing expert will also ensure your designs meet safety standards and are suitable for construction in Oregon.",
